WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.920 --> 00:00:06.100
If you are a hand knitter,
you may have heard of an I-Cord bind off.

2
00:00:06.130 --> 00:00:10.220
Let's work it on the knitting machine.

3
00:00:10.250 --> 00:00:14.180
Let's say you need to bind off an edge
and you want a decorative finish.

4
00:00:14.210 --> 00:00:18.100
But you don't want a band
or a hem or crochet.

5
00:00:18.130 --> 00:00:21.540
You want something a little bit different.

6
00:00:21.570 --> 00:00:26.460
Maybe you've knit a sleeve from the top
down, or you want to finish off the top

7
00:00:26.490 --> 00:00:30.700
of a pocket, or even
the neck edge of a sweater.

8
00:00:30.730 --> 00:00:35.060
Begin the bindoff by removing
the live stitches on waste yarn.

9
00:00:35.090 --> 00:00:39.900
Use a good, contrasting color
to make things easier for yourself.

10
00:00:39.930 --> 00:00:44.000
Cast on three stitches

11
00:00:46.280 --> 00:00:49.860
and set your machine
to slip to the right only.

12
00:00:49.890 --> 00:00:52.940
Now this works on any knitting machine.

13
00:00:52.970 --> 00:00:57.500
Push the carriage to the left
and knit the first row of the I-Cord.

14
00:00:57.530 --> 00:01:02.120
Then push the carriage to the right
and slip the three stitches. You'll have

15
00:01:02.150 --> 00:01:04.260
knit one row.

16
00:01:04.290 --> 00:01:07.130
I'm assuming you know how to knit I-Cord.

17
00:01:07.160 --> 00:01:11.460
If not, check the Knit It Now
video on the subject.

18
00:01:11.490 --> 00:01:15.980
With the private or the wrong
side of your garment facing you.

19
00:01:16.010 --> 00:01:22.260
Pick up the first garment stitch
and hang it on the first I-Cord Needle.

20
00:01:22.290 --> 00:01:24.850
All of this is done on the side opposite

21
00:01:24.880 --> 00:01:26.960
of the carriage.

22
00:01:35.720 --> 00:01:38.980
So knit over and slip back.

23
00:01:39.010 --> 00:01:42.210
This binds off the first stitch.

24
00:01:42.240 --> 00:01:46.930
Pick up the next stitch and hang
it on that same needle.

25
00:01:46.960 --> 00:01:49.740
There will always be three
needles in working position.

26
00:01:49.770 --> 00:01:53.100
Don't increase any.

27
00:01:53.130 --> 00:01:56.780
Pick up the next stitch,
knit over and back.

28
00:01:56.810 --> 00:01:59.700
Repeat this across the entire piece,

29
00:01:59.730 --> 00:02:06.400
automatically forming I-Cord and binding
off the stitches at the same time.

30
00:02:23.960 --> 00:02:26.100
Here's something to keep in mind when you

31
00:02:26.130 --> 00:02:31.140
work this technique.
You're adding rows to stitches.

32
00:02:31.170 --> 00:02:34.730
Stitches are generally wider than rows,

33
00:02:34.760 --> 00:02:40.730
so you may need to knit the I-Cord at a
looser tension than the garment tension.

34
00:02:40.760 --> 00:02:47.680
Try this bind off on a swatch with your
yarn before working your final piece.

35
00:02:49.560 --> 00:02:53.260
When you remove the waste yarn voila,

36
00:02:53.290 --> 00:02:57.880
you have a neat and tidy edge.

37
00:03:03.960 --> 00:03:08.380
Now let's turn things around
and work an I-Cord cast on.

38
00:03:08.410 --> 00:03:11.060
Bet you haven't heard of this one before.

39
00:03:11.090 --> 00:03:17.500
The purpose of any cast on method is
to create a closed edge for our knitting.

40
00:03:17.530 --> 00:03:23.760
This technique is decorative
and it looks great on both sides.

41
00:03:24.040 --> 00:03:29.580
Knit a length of cord a little bit
wider than your desired width.

42
00:03:29.610 --> 00:03:35.740
Then carefully hang a single strand
from each row of the eye I-Cord.

43
00:03:35.770 --> 00:03:38.540
Now be careful to pick up along a single

44
00:03:38.570 --> 00:03:43.880
column of stitches so you
don't twist the cord.

45
00:03:44.080 --> 00:03:46.500
This time we're picking up stitches

46
00:03:46.530 --> 00:03:53.220
to rows and you'll want to pick up
approximately three stitches

47
00:03:53.250 --> 00:04:00.100
hanging on three needles
for every four rows of the cord.

48
00:04:00.130 --> 00:04:01.580
That's it.

49
00:04:01.610 --> 00:04:03.360
Just start knitting and you'll have

50
00:04:03.390 --> 00:04:07.820
a pretty closed edge that looks
great on both sides.

51
00:04:07.850 --> 00:04:13.360
Again, all of this can
be done on any machine.

52
00:04:15.600 --> 00:04:17.980
Let's take this one step further.

53
00:04:18.010 --> 00:04:24.140
Why not use I-Cord  as an embellishment?
Instead of attaching this little lover's

54
00:04:24.160 --> 00:04:30.300
knot after the garment is finished,
knit and knot the I-Cord first.

55
00:04:30.330 --> 00:04:32.740
When you get to the desired location,

56
00:04:32.770 --> 00:04:37.100
remove the knitting from the machine
on waste yarn or a garter bar.

57
00:04:37.130 --> 00:04:44.540
Hang the I-Cord on the needles.
Rehang your work and continue knitting.

58
00:04:44.570 --> 00:04:46.900
Get creative with I-Cord.

59
00:04:46.920 --> 00:04:51.500
Use contrasting colors or novelty yarns.

60
00:04:51.530 --> 00:04:55.100
Once you try an ICORD,
cast on and bind off.

61
00:04:55.130 --> 00:04:58.720
Sure, you'll find lots of other
ways to use these techniques.

